I didn't do a complete set of suggestions, partly because, as I
explained, I consider it trivial, and partly because, with NH4
supporting key remapping now, it's largely moot.

However, since you ask...

asdw: move west, south, east, or north, respectively
ASDW: move until something stops you.
qezx: move diagonally
QEZX: move diagonally until something stops you
cC:   move diagonally southeast (same as x and X)
u:    use a wand (like zap in vanilla)
u:    use a tool or other thing that is currently (a)pplied
U:    Use magical knowledge to cast a spell
p:    Pack: list the contents of your pack
l:    Look: does both (:)nearlook and (s)earch at the same time
L:    Loot an open container on the floor
?:    Help: same as in vanilla
i:    Ingest: eat or drink, used for comestables, potions, and
              anything else that goes into your mouth
I:    Inscribe: like engrave in vanilla
p:    Put-on: covers both wear and put on
t:    Take off: covers (r)emove, (t)ake off, and (A)rmor
r:    read a scroll or spellbook
P:    Prepare a weapon (like wield in vanilla)
j:    Jettison: leave a single item behind
J:    Jettison: divest yourself of many items
f:    Fire: fire a weapon from your quiver, as in vanilla
m:    move, same as in vanilla
T:    Throw an object
F:    Fight, same as in vanilla
k:    kick
o:    Open (or unlock if that's necessary)
O:    close or lock (opposite of o)
K:    Kloze (close, or locK if it's already closed)
N:    Name a monster, type of object, or individual object

#break a wand (not used often enough to justify a key)
#pay (not used often enough to justify a key)
#configure (set game options)
#exploremode (not used often enough to justify a key)
#quiver (not used often enough to justify a key)
#quit (saves your game, like (S)ave in vanilla)
#abandon (quit without saving; "Are you sure?")

It took me all of fifteen minutes to come up with this.  It's
just not that hard.  The English language has vocabulary coming
out the everliving royal wazootie, so when the first word that
springs to mind starts with a letter that's already taken, there
is virtually always a synonym available.  The only keys above
that are even slightly awkward are K and O (which do exactly the
same thing, so you have your choice between them).

Note too that nothing is assigned to y or Y, so accidentally
holding down a key a little too long does not result in an
automatic yes.  Nothing is assigned to lowercase n, either, and N
prompts you with a menu and uses no time if you cancel it.  In
vanilla, I find myself avoiding northwest as a movement direction
because it's dangerous.  That's sloppy interface design.

